{
  "question": "Ozempic pen is preferred, but the patient takes Januvia and has commercial insurance. Do we need PA, and who gets billed first?",
  "status": "answered",
  "assumption": "Assuming NY Medicaid (patient has Medicaid plus commercial insurance as TPL); the question didn't name the program.",
  "answer": "Assuming NY Medicaid with commercial as TPL:\n\n1. **Bill the commercial plan first.** Medicaid is payer of last resort; a claim sent to Medicaid first denies with NCPDP 717 (\"Client Has Other Insurance\"). Bypassing the TPL is treated as fraudulent billing. Keep the TPL denial/payment records for 6 years.\n2. **Then bill Medicaid as secondary** for patient responsibility. A Medicaid PA does not replace the TPL's obligation.\n3. **PA:** Ozempic pen is preferred on the PDL, but PA is required when a GLP-1 and a DPP-4 (Januvia) are used concurrently. Januvia is listed as preferred (BLTG/DO). The prescriber, not the pharmacy, submits the PA (ePA via CoverMyMeds can be initiated by the pharmacy). Preferred/non-preferred changes take effect 10/22/2026.\n\nThe commercial plan's own PA rules aren't in these documents.",
